\end{figure}

For the full program, the lattice is the Cartesian product $\mathcal{L}_c^{|\mathit{Vars}|}$, ordered pointwise.

\subsection{Transfer Functions}\label{sec:cp-transfer}

\begin{definition}[Constant Propagation Transfer Functions]\label{def:cp-transfer}
For a statement $s$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $x = c$ (constant assignment): $\sigma[x \mapsto c]$, all other variables unchanged.
  \item $x = y \mathbin{\text{op}} z$: 
    \[
    \sigma[x \mapsto \begin{cases}
      \sigma(y) \mathbin{\text{op}} \sigma(z) & \text{if } \sigma(y), \sigma(z) \in \mathbb{Z} \\
      \top & \text{if } \sigma(y) = \top \text{ or } \sigma(z) = \top \\
      \bot & \text{if } \sigma(y) = \bot \text{ or } \sigma(z) = \bot
    \end{cases}]
    \]
  \item $x = \mathit{input}()$: $\sigma[x \mapsto \top]$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Meet Operator}\label{sec:cp-meet}

The meet operator for the constant lattice is:
\[
c_1 \sqcap c_2 = \begin{cases}
  c_1 & \text{if } c_1 = c_2 \\
  \top & \text{if } c_1 \neq c_2 \text{ and both are constants} \\
  c_1 & \text{if } c_2 = \bot \\
  c_2 & \text{if } c_1 = \bot \\
  \top & \text{if either is } \top
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{insight}
Constant propagation is the canonical example of a non-distributive analysis. The transfer function for $x = y + z$ does not distribute over the meet: if path 1 gives $(y=3, z=4)$ and path 2 gives $(y=5, z=2)$, the meet gives $(y=\top, z=\top)$, and the transfer function yields $x = \top$. But the MOP solution would compute $x = 7$ on path 1 and $x = 7$ on path 2, yielding $x = 7$. The MFP solution loses precision because it first merges the values of $y$ and $z$ and then applies the transfer function.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Kildall's Method}\label{sec:kildall}

Kildall's method~\cite{kildall1973unified} was the first to recognize that constant propagation could be formulated as a dataflow analysis over the constant lattice. The algorithm maintains a mapping from variables to lattice values at each program point and iterates until a fixedpoint is reached.

\section{Implementation Patterns}\label{sec:implementation-patterns}

\subsection{Generic Dataflow Engine}\label{sec:generic-engine}

All the analyses described above share a common structure. A well-designed compiler implements a \emph{generic dataflow engine} that is parameterized by:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The lattice (type of dataflow facts, meet/join operators, top/bottom elements)
  \item The direction (forward or backward)
  \item The transfer function (gen/kill sets or more complex functions)
  \item The combination rule (meet or join at merge points)
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Generic Dataflow Framework}
\label{alg:generic-df}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Direction $d \in \{\text{forward}, \text{backward}\}$
\Require Lattice $(\mathcal{L}, \sqcap, \sqcup, \top, \bot)$
\Require Transfer functions $\{f_n\}_{n \in N}$
\Require Combination operator $\bigotimes \in \{\sqcap, \sqcup\}$
\State Initialize boundary conditions
\State Initialize all other values to $\top$ (for must) or $\bot$ (for may)
\State $\mathit{worklist} \gets$ all nodes in appropriate order
\While{$\mathit{worklist} \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $n \gets$ dequeue from $\mathit{worklist}$
  \State $\mathit{new\_in} \gets \bigotimes_{m \in \mathit{neighbors}(n)} \mathit{out}[m]$
  \State $\mathit{new\_out} \gets f_n(\mathit{new\_in})$
  \If{$\mathit{new\_out} \neq \mathit{out}[n]$}
    \State $\mathit{out}[n] \gets \mathit{new\_out}$
    \ForAll{$s \in \mathit{successors\_or\_predecessors}(n)$}
      \State Enqueue $s$ if not already in $\mathit{worklist}$
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Bit-Vector Implementation}\label{sec:bitvector}

For set-based analyses (reaching definitions, available expressions, live variables), the lattice elements are sets, and the most efficient representation is a \emph{bit vector}. Each element of the universal set is assigned an index, and membership is represented by a single bit.

\begin{itemize}
  \item Union $\cup$ becomes bitwise OR
  \item Intersection $\cap$ becomes bitwise AND
  \item Set difference $\setminus$ becomes bitwise AND-NOT
  \item Membership test becomes a bit test
\end{itemize}

This representation is extremely efficient: a set of $n$ elements requires $\lceil n / w \rceil$ words (where $w$ is the machine word size), and all operations are performed in parallel at the hardware level.

\subsection{Sparse Evaluation}\label{sec:sparse-eval}

For many analyses, most program points have the same dataflow value. \emph{Sparse evaluation} exploits this by computing dataflow information only at points where it changes---typically the $\phi$-nodes in SSA form. This can dramatically reduce the cost of dataflow analysis for large programs.

\begin{definition}[Sparse Problem]\label{def:sparse-problem}
A dataflow problem is \textbf{sparse} if the transfer function for most nodes is the identity function: $f_n(x) = x$. Only at a small number of \emph{interesting} nodes does the transfer function produce a different value.
\end{definition}

