\section{Summary}\label{sec:forward-backward-summary}

The classical dataflow analyses---reaching definitions, available expressions, live variables, very busy expressions, and constant propagation---demonstrate the power and versatility of the dataflow framework. They differ in direction (forward or backward), combination type (may or must), and lattice structure (powerset or product), but all share the same underlying algorithmic structure. The key theoretical result is that for distributive analyses, the practical iterative algorithm computes the ideal MOP solution exactly. For non-distributive analyses like constant propagation, the MFP solution is a sound but potentially imprecise approximation---a limitation that motivates the more sophisticated techniques of abstract interpretation developed in later chapters.
